  <div class="mt-20 grid gap-12 sm:grid-cols-2 sm:gap-16">
    <section>
      <h2 class="page-display mb-4 mt-0 text-3xl sm:text-4xl">Deeper means calmer.</h2>
      <p class="page-prose">Equalise early, turn at the depth you planned, and come up with air to spare.</p>
      <p class="page-prose mb-0">I certify Molchanovs Wave 1 and Wave 2. Always train with a qualified buddy watching and stay well within your limits.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 class="page-display mb-4 mt-0 text-3xl sm:text-4xl">One-to-one for kids.</h2>
      <p class="page-prose mb-0">An instructor stays beside your child the whole time, and each skill is solid before the next one starts. <a href="/junior/" class="font-bold decoration-sky-200 decoration-8">Freediving for kids</a></p>
    </section>
  </div>

  <div class="essay-wrap">
  <article class="essay">
    <section id="twelve">
      <h2 class="page-display">First contact.</h2>
      <p class="page-prose">I first tried freediving in December 2023. The rule turned out to be simple: the more relaxed you are, the longer and easier you stay under. Once I understood that you need to unplug in your head, I held my breath for over four minutes.</p>
      <p class="page-prose">Depth shows you how calm you really are. Back then I turned around at twelve metres.</p>
      <p class="page-prose">I kept wondering about the people who dive past a hundred metres on a single breath. Who are they? How do you stay relaxed that deep?</p>
    </section>
    <section id="breath">
      <h2 class="page-display">The urge to breathe.</h2>
      <p class="page-prose">Before Deep Week Thailand in 2024 I spent weeks training breath-holds on dry land. Why does it feel like suffocating so soon? You are not short of oxygen yet. What you feel is carbon dioxide building up, and the first urges arrive within a minute.</p>
      <p class="page-prose">Let the alarming pictures in your head go by, and you can hold a little longer. It feels strange because you expect imminent doom and nothing is happening. Then it turns into meditation, a way to calm down the body and mind together. Your body becomes a suit you are only wearing.</p>
      <p class="page-prose">What counts is how fast carbon dioxide climbs. Fifteen seconds of hard finning can leave me gasping sooner than a long, still hold. So I work on moving smoothly, without sudden moves or wasted effort. Trust the water and it stops pushing back.</p>
    </section>
    <section id="letting-go">
      <h2 class="page-display">Letting go.</h2>
      <p class="page-prose">At Deep Week Thailand in 2024 it finally clicked. Freediving resets you from the inside. You cannot dive angry, hurt or irritated.</p>
      <p class="page-prose">Freediving is letting go of everything. Every dive should feel easy and pleasant, and so should life. Dive like that and you find yourself deep without noticing how you got there.</p>
      <p class="page-prose">A year earlier I had left every grudge I carried on the seabed off Racha Yai. Free as in freedom.</p>
    </section>
    <section id="wall">
      <h2 class="page-display">The wall.</h2>
      <p class="page-prose">To qualify as an instructor, you swim a hundred metres underwater on one breath. Four lengths of the pool. After three, I hit a wall.</p>
      <p class="page-prose">Seventy-five you can force: clench your teeth, speed up, drag yourself to the end. A hundred you cannot. Willpower runs out, and the only way through is to relax.</p>
      <p class="page-prose">Before my last attempt I sat on the edge of the pool, bracing for certain death. I tried to slow my pulse and it only climbed. My friend Misha said: &ldquo;It is not just you. Seventy-five to a hundred is the hardest stretch. After a hundred it gets easier.&rdquo;</p>
      <p class="page-prose">I pushed off with one sentence in my head: this is the worst part, and nothing is going to happen. I reached a hundred, came up, and the wall was gone.</p>
      <p class="page-prose">Certain death turned out to be nothing more than sensations in the body. You cannot argue the body out of them. It has to live through the moment and learn: I met this, and nothing happened. The wall is a process. You learn to pass through fear.</p>
    </section>
    <section id="thoughts">
      <h2 class="page-display">Thoughts that are not yours.</h2>
      <p class="page-prose">Diving in the open ocean, with no bottom and nothing to fix your eyes on, is easier than poking around wrecks. Around you there is only blue. Then you take in the scale, realise there are tens of metres of water above you, and thoughts start seeping in. They are not yours.</p>
      <p class="page-prose">You live inside a body with a mind of its own. It keeps broadcasting its moods, and you take them for your own thoughts.</p>
      <p class="page-prose">This is where the inner work begins: settling your nervous system. Nothing is happening. All is well.</p>
    </section>
    <section id="water">
      <h2 class="page-display">Only the water.</h2>
      <p class="page-prose">In May 2026 I passed my last instructor test at Deep Week Bali: thirty metres with no fins, back up without touching the line. After that, the pressure was off. The forty metres I used to push myself through on Phuket became light and calm. I stopped trying, counting and controlling, and everything I had trained fell into place.</p>
      <p class="page-prose">We spend our whole lives wrapped in a thin film of fear. In Bali it burst. I fell head first and nothing happened. No fear, no discomfort, no me. Sunlight, blue, ocean. Only the water, gently holding me.</p>
    </section>
    <section id="land">
      <h2 class="page-display">On land.</h2>
      <p class="page-prose">Depth no longer worries me, so I can turn inward and scan my body for whatever is still tense.</p>
      <p class="page-prose">These days I mostly dive with students. I follow them down, watch what they do, and look for what else I can let go of, so I spend less energy and get less tired. Every dive is a lesson. I keep finding things inside me that I missed while my attention was pointed outwards.</p>
      <p class="page-prose">Life works the same way. It is easier when nothing bothers you, and that can be trained.</p>
      <p class="page-prose">It was all in my head. It was only discomfort.</p>
    </section>
  </article>
